Ours.  The RTG layer's card interface: rtg.h gains rtgCardOps, a versioned
record a card driver fills and the layer drives it through, and the card
table rtgCards[], so that more than one card can sit under the one layer.

- the record: the card's name, device and mode list; its caps (depth,
  bits per pixel, the TrueColor masks, bits per RGB value, -blitmin's
  default, whether its registers are mapped into the server); probe,
  setMode, modeSize, initHW, closeHW; fillBox and copyBox (one box; the
  layer orders the boxes of a region); flush; monitorSwitch;
- the contract: fillBox and copyBox may queue and return early, flush
  waits until the card has finished, and the layer flushes before any CPU
  access the queue could meet and before every screen entry point
  returns; a hook that returns FALSE has taken nothing and the layer draws
  on the CPU; a card that loses its blitter draws what it holds with
  rtgCpuFill/rtgCpuCopy and sets rtgBlitMin to 0;
- the monitor switch is a card operation: the ZZ9000 driver reads its
  argument as an int, the VA2000 driver as an unsigned short (an int sent
  to it reads 0 on m68k);
- rtgScreenRec gains `queued', so a flush with nothing queued is one
  compare.
Only the header: the next patch moves the layer and the ZZ9000 driver onto
it.  MIT; the header's three holders as before.

